<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thomas Palaiologos</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1409">1409</a>–<a href="/years/1465">1465</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palaiologos-dynasty">Palaiologos dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thomas Palaiologos was a Byzantine prince, youngest brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-xi-palaiologos">Constantine XI</a>, and despot of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Morea</a> from 1428. He completed the conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a> in 1430–1432 and ruled the Peloponnese jointly with his brother Demetrios. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> overran the Morea in 1460 he fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, placing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monemvasia">Monemvasia</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-ii">Pope Pius II</a>, and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1465.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thomas-palaiologos.jpg" alt="Thomas Palaiologos" width="320" height="353" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thomas_Palaiologos2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1430 Centurione agreed to marry his daughter Catherine to <b>Thomas Palaiologos</b>, with the principality as her dowry, keeping only his castle of Arcadia (Kyparissia) in Messenia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-achaea">Principality of Achaea · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After his death in 1432 <b>Thomas Palaiologos</b> took it over, ending Latin rule in the region.</blockquote><a href="/state/barony-of-arcadia">Barony of Arcadia</a></li><li><blockquote>When Mehmed II conquered the Morea in 1460, the despot <b>Thomas Palaiologos</b> handed it to Pope Pius II.</blockquote><a href="/state/venetian-monemvasia">Venetian Monemvasia</a></li></ul></section>
